IZ4 dosh.do

# Every IZ4 carries the foundation word for word: human intention protected.
# It begins with Invariant Zero, humans first: https://iz4.you/foundation. It is the same in every IZ4 and not yours to edit.
# Your own invariants are named under your project's domain.

IS FOR WHAT?  dosh ("do shell") turns a plain-language request typed at the
              shell into a suggested shell command. You say what you want in
              your own words; dosh asks an LLM for one command that would do it,
              shows you that command along with an explanation of what it does
              and a warning if it would change anything, and then asks whether
              to run it. Nothing runs unless you say yes. It is a way to get at
              the power of the shell without remembering the exact incantation,
              while keeping the person at the keyboard in charge of what
              actually executes.

IS FOR WHO?   People at a terminal who know what they want to do but not the
              exact shell command.

PROMISE request-in-plain-words.dosh.do

  WHEN      ordinary words are typed as the arguments to dosh at the shell
  ALWAYS    Natural language is the interface. The request is ordinary words
            given directly as the arguments to dosh, with no query syntax or
            quoting ritual to learn; a handful of reserved words (config,
            prompt, help, version) are subcommands, and everything else is
            treated as the request itself.
  PROTECTS  the person's ability to ask without a syntax to learn

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE user-chooses-the-llm.dosh.do

  WHEN      an LLM is chosen and configured to answer dosh's requests
  ALWAYS    Which LLM is used, and how, is the user's choice: dosh exposes its
            LLM settings through configuration rather than hard-wiring a
            provider. dosh's value is the workflow around the model, not any one
            model.
  PROTECTS  the user's choice of which model answers them

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE nothing-runs-unconfirmed.dosh.do

  WHEN      a suggested command is put to the person to run or not
  ALWAYS    Nothing executes without explicit confirmation. The suggested
            command is run only after the person answers the confirmation prompt
            affirmatively; any other answer, including an empty one, means the
            command is not run. There is no mode, flag or shortcut that skips
            this.
  PROTECTS  the person's say over what executes on their machine

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE exact-command-shown-first.dosh.do

  WHEN      a suggested command is shown before the person decides to run it
  ALWAYS    The person sees the exact command before deciding. The full command
            text, its explanation, and any warning are shown first.
  PROTECTS  The decision is made on what will actually run rather than on a
            paraphrase of the request.

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE changes-carry-a-warning.dosh.do

  WHEN      a suggested command would change the system rather than only read it
  ALWAYS    A command that changes the system is announced as such. When the
            suggestion is not read-only, a short warning describing the risk is
            shown alongside it; silently presenting a destructive command as
            ordinary is a defect.
  PROTECTS  the person's awareness of the risk before they run it

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE safe-when-unclear.dosh.do

  WHEN      a request's intent is unclear when the command is chosen
  ALWAYS    Safety is preferred over ambition when intent is unclear. Read-only
            substitutes are favoured, elevated privileges are not assumed, and
            destructive flags are not used by default.
  PROTECTS  the person's system from damage done on a guess

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE commands-stay-local.dosh.do

  WHEN      a suggested command would reach beyond this machine
  ALWAYS    Suggested commands stay local. dosh does not suggest commands that
            reach out to external services, APIs, or http/https URLs; the
            request goes to the LLM, the command acts on this machine.
  PROTECTS  what is on the person's machine staying on it

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE never-invents-a-command.dosh.do

  WHEN      the LLM returns no usable command and explanation
  ALWAYS    Nothing is invented when the LLM gives nothing usable. If no command
            and explanation come back, dosh fails loudly rather than guessing or
            running anything.
  PROTECTS  the person's trust that nothing shown is a guess

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

PROMISE fits-this-machine.dosh.do

  WHEN      a request is translated into a command for the machine dosh runs on
  ALWAYS    Suggestions fit the machine in front of you. The request is
            translated with knowledge of the operating system, kernel and
            hardware dosh is running on.
  PROTECTS  the person's command fitting the system it will run on

  CHANGES REQUIRE signed human approval for the exact change
  CHECKED WITH    linked tests; failures block commit

